{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H9EpwJTGyqndrRfMfTtTUt","type":"tool_result","content":"30\t\\title{How Many Comparisons Change the Leader? Refit-Verified Influence Perturbations of Bradley--Terry Leaderboards Across Evaluation Designs}\n31\t\\iclrfinalcopy\n32\t\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n33\t\n34\t\\begin{document}\n35\t\\maketitle\n36\t\n37\t\\begin{abstract}\n38\tLeaderboards for language models and sports rank participants by fitting a Bradley--Terry (BT) model to pairwise comparisons. It is rarely measured how much of the comparison data a reported ranking depends on. We measure the smallest perturbation we can find and certify that changes the leader or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard. A perturbation either drops comparisons or reverses their outcomes. The search orders comparisons by their first-order influence on the decisive score margin, and an exact refit verifies every reported size. We evaluate 15 cases spanning crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena), expert and GPT-4-judge annotations (MT-Bench), and two NBA seasons. In every design, tens of comparisons change the leader: 62 of 135,622 Arena votes (0.046\\%), 92 expert and 143 GPT-4 judgments, and 7 and 16 NBA games. These sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations, and identical across seeds. The most fragile design depends on the scale and the target. As a fraction of the data Arena is the most fragile; by count, NBA is. Moving a top-$k$ boundary can cost one game, or more than changing the leader. All sizes are upper bounds; cross-design differences are descriptive, not causal.\n39\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n40\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n41\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n42\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n43\t\\end{abstract}\n44\t\n45\t\\section{Introduction}\n46\t\\label{sec:intro}\n47\t\n48\tPairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n49\t\n50\tWe ask: \\emph{how small a verified perturbation of the comparison data changes the top-1 or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard? How does that size differ between crowdsourced votes, expert annotation, an LLM judge, and sports results?} We answer with a measurement instrument, not a new estimator. Following the drop-data robustness metric of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}, we order comparisons by a one-step influence approximation~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP} of their effect on the decisive score margin. We find the shortest prefix of that ordering whose removal (or outcome reversal) changes the target. An exact refit checks every candidate. Each reported size is therefore a certified \\emph{upper bound} on the minimal perturbation, not an approximation.\n51\t\n52\tOur central finding has two parts. First, in every design we study, tens of comparisons are enough to change the leader. The verified sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations of the same data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}). Second, the question ``which design is most fragile?'' has no scale-free answer. Measured as a fraction of the data, the crowdsourced Arena leader is the most fragile (0.046\\%) and the GPT-4-judge leader the least (5.96\\%). Measured as a count, the NBA leaders are the most fragile (7 and 16 games). The target matters as much as the scale. In both NBA seasons, a single game changes the top-5 set. In MT-Bench, by contrast, the top-3 set costs more than the leader.\n53\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n54\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_drop_frac]]\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n57\t(i) A refit-verified, influence-ordered protocol for leaderboard fragility. It covers two perturbation types (dropping and flipping comparisons) and two targets (top-1 and top-$k$ set). It is validated against a planted exact minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, \\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n58\t(ii) A cross-design measurement on held-out snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n59\t(iii) Three regularities with stated limits. Flipping always needs fewer comparisons than dropping. The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper or dearer to move than the leader. Fragility rankings across designs reverse between the count and fraction scales (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n60\t(iv) An explicit negative result: the search procedure we evaluate does not improve on the plain influence ordering. The pre-declared test of that improvement is unsupported, so we make no algorithmic-superiority claim (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n61\t\n62\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n63\t\\centering\n64\t\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n65\t\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n66\t\\label{fig:overview}\n67\t\\end{figure}\n68\t\n69\t\\section{Related Work}\n70\t\\label{sec:related}\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Paired-comparison models and ranking.}\n73\tThe BT model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} and its extensions to ties~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET} underlie most comparison leaderboards. Ties are often handled, as here, by counting them as half wins. Maximum-likelihood fitting is classical~\\citep{Hunter2003MMAF}. Spectral and counting alternatives come with sample-complexity guarantees for recovering the top-$K$ items~\\citep{Negahban2012RankCR,Chen2015SpectralMT,Chen2017SPECTRALMA,Shah2015SimpleRA}. Elo ratings play the same role in sports and games~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. Uncertainty for BT scores is usually quantified by asymptotic theory~\\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI} or the bootstrap~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA}, and the robustness of Elo-style rankings of language models has itself been examined~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}. These tools answer ``how variable is the ranking under resampling?''. We ask a worst-case, data-specific question: ``how few concrete comparisons does the ranking rest on?''.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Influence and drop-data robustness.}\n76\tInfluence diagnostics go back to Cook's distance~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI}. They were extended to nonlinear models~\\citep{Monari2000WithdrawingAE} and to modern machine learning~\\citep{Koh2017UnderstandingBP}, though they can be fragile for deep networks~\\citep{Basu2020InfluenceFI}. Infinitesimal-jackknife approximations make leave-out analysis cheap~\\citep{Giordano2018ASA}, and higher-order influence expansions refine it~\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}. \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} turn this machinery into a robustness metric, the approximate maximum influence perturbation: the smallest fraction of data whose removal can overturn a conclusion. For ordinary least squares in low dimensions, such drop-data conclusions can be audited with provable guarantees~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We adopt the drop-data question, apply it to rank conclusions of a BT fit, add an outcome-reversal perturbation, and certify every size by exact refits rather than reporting the linear approximation.\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Manipulation and validity of LLM leaderboards.}\n79\tChatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. On the estimation side, \\citet{Patil2020RankAF} study ranking recovery under BTL-type models when an adversary corrupts a fraction of the pairwise comparisons, which is the setting our flip perturbation instantiates on fixed real data. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n80\t\n81\t\\section{Problem Setup}\n82\t\\label{sec:setup}\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Data and estimator.}\n85\tA case consists of $n$ comparison rows $r=(a_r,b_r,y_r)$ among $p$ players. The outcome is $y_r\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$: a win for $a_r$, a tie, or a win for $b_r$. The leaderboard is the ridge-penalized BT maximum-likelihood estimate\n86\t\\begin{equation}\n87\t\\hat\\theta=\\arg\\max_{\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^p}\\;\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\Big[y_r\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{a_r}-\\theta_{b_r})+(1-y_r)\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{b_r}-\\theta_{a_r})\\Big]-\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\theta\\|_2^2 ,\n88\t\\label{eq:bt}\n89\t\\end{equation}\n90\twhere $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Targets.}\n93\tLet $S$ be the original top-$k$ set under $\\hat\\theta$. The \\emph{top-$k$ target changes} after a perturbation if the refit $\\tilde\\theta$ satisfies $\\max_{j\\notin S}\\tilde\\theta_j>\\min_{i\\in S}\\tilde\\theta_i+\\tau$, with $\\tau=10^{-6}$. Top-1 is the special case $k=1$: some other player overtakes the leader. We study top-1 for every source, plus top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench, which has few players.\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Perturbations.}\n96\tA \\emph{drop} perturbation deletes a set $D$ of rows. A \\emph{flip} perturbation reverses the outcomes of a set $F$ of decided rows ($y\\mapsto 1-y$). Ties cannot be flipped. Drops model missing or filtered data and targeted removal. Flips model mislabeled or adversarially cast votes: the data volume stays the same and only the verdicts change. The quantity of interest is the minimal size $k^\\star$ of a set that changes the target. We report $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$, the size of a set we construct and verify.\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Two size scales.}\n99\tWe report every size both as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$, and we argue that neither alone is adequate. The fraction is the natural robustness scale of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}. It answers ``what share of the evidence must be wrong?'', which is the relevant question for random noise and data-quality concerns. The count answers ``how many comparisons must an adversary control?'', which is the relevant question for manipulation, where cost scales with votes, not with shares. The two scales can order designs differently when $n$ varies by two orders of magnitude, as it does here. We treat that disagreement as a finding, not a nuisance (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n100\t\n101\t\\section{Method: Refit-Verified Influence Ordering}\n102\t\\label{sec:method}\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{Influence of one row on the decisive margin.}\n105\tLet $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin are therefore\n106\t\\begin{equation}\n107\t\\Delta^{\\text{drop}}_r=-(y_r-p_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}),\\qquad\n108\t\\Delta^{\\text{flip}}_r=(1-2y_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}).\n109\t\\label{eq:influence}\n110\t\\end{equation}\n111\tRows are sorted by ascending $\\Delta_r$, so the rows that shrink the margin most come first. One linear solve gives $v$; the ordering costs $O(p^3+n\\log n)$. Equation~\\eqref{eq:influence} also predicts two qualitative regularities that we test in \\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}. First, for a row won by $a_r$ ($y_r=1$), the flip effect exceeds the drop effect by the factor $1/(1-p_r)\\ge 1$, which equals 2 for an even match-up. Flips should therefore need fewer rows. Second, $|v_{a}-v_{b}|$ shrinks as the information about the $L$--$C$ contrast grows. The \\emph{count} needed should thus scale with the margin times that information, while the \\emph{fraction} also falls when the rows touching $L$ and $C$ are a small share of a large data set.\n112\t\n113\t\\paragraph{Exact verification by prefix search.}\n114\tThe linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$, and the target condition is tested on the result. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is a single NumPy module (Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\n115\t\n116\t\\paragraph{What a verified size means.}\n117\t$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings such as low-dimensional least squares~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. Even there, exact search and certificates that no smaller set exists become computationally demanding beyond a few dimensions, while greedy and influence-based heuristics only exhibit sets and therefore give upper bounds~\\citep{Freund2023TowardsPR}. We do not claim to solve the problem on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n118\t\n119\t\\paragraph{Comparators.}\n120\tTwo comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Validity controls.}\n123\t\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Data and protocol.}\n126\tTable~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams, following the long use of BT models for sports tournaments~\\citep{Cattelan2013DynamicBM}. We fit a static BT model to each season, without the time-varying strengths such models can include. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n127\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n128\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n129\t\n130\t\\begin{table}[t]\n131\t\\centering\n132\t\\caption{Evaluation designs and cases. $n$ is the number of comparison rows after filtering, recovered exactly from the evaluator's reported fraction $\\kver/n$ and the verified size $\\kver$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}). Every source contributes drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$ cases.}\n133\t\\label{tab:cases}\n134\t\\small\n135\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrcl@{}}\n136\t\\toprule\n137\tDesign & Source (pinned snapshot) & $n$ & top-$k$ & What a row is \\\\\n138\t\\midrule\n139\tCrowdsourced votes & Chatbot Arena human pref.\\ (140k) & 135{,}622 & 5 & one user vote \\\\\n140\tExpert annotation & MT-Bench human judgments & 3{,}355 & 3 & one expert judgment \\\\\n141\tLLM judge & MT-Bench GPT-4 pair judgments & 2{,}400 & 3 & one GPT-4 verdict \\\\\n142\tSports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\n143\tSports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n144\t\\midrule\n145\tControl & planted 3-player case (synthetic) & -- & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\n146\tControl & Arena drop/top-1 with rows permuted & 135{,}622 & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\n147\t\\bottomrule\n148\t\\end{tabular}\n149\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n150\t\\end{table}\n151\t\n152\t\\section{Results}\n153\t\\label{sec:results}\n154\t\n155\t\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\n156\t\\label{sec:res-validity}\n157\t\n158\tTable~\\ref{tab:validity} collects the evidence that the measured sizes mean what we claim. On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. This score is $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$, so a value of 0 means the instrument returned exactly the brute-force minimum. Here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n159\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.synthetic_control_score]]\n160\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.permutation_control_score]]\n161\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_match]]\n162\t\n163\tAgainst the random comparator, the influence-ordered sets are far smaller. The mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{rand}}/\\kver)$ is 3.975, with 95\\% CI [3.934, 4.0]. This corresponds to a geometric-mean factor of about $15.7\\times$. The upper end of the interval sits at the clip value of 4, so most cases are censored, and the true advantage is \\emph{at least} this large. Fragility to chosen perturbations is therefore a different quantity from fragility to noise. A leader that survives typical random corruption of tens of comparisons can still fall to a targeted set of the same size.\n164\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n165\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#random_ratio_x]]\n166\t\n167\tAgainst the direct heuristic, the influence ordering was never worse in the Arena, GPT-4-judge, and NBA families: each family score is 0. In the MT-Bench-expert family, the score of $-0.0053$ shows that the heuristic was marginally smaller in at least one case. The evaluator records this only at family level. The ordering therefore improves on the obvious ``remove the leader's wins'' attack in most cases, but not uniformly.\n168\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n169\t\n170\t\\paragraph{No improvement over the plain influence ordering.}\n171\tThe configuration evaluated here \\emph{is} the influence ordering plus verified prefix search. Its sizes coincide with those of the evaluator's own one-step-influence comparator in every case: the mean $\\log_2$ ratio is 0.0, with CI [0.0, 0.0]. The top-1 drop fractions are equal to the reported precision. The pre-declared test of a strict improvement over this ordering (CI lower bound $>0$) is therefore \\emph{not supported}. We make no claim of algorithmic superiority. Refinements that relinearize the influence after partial removals, or that prune and swap rows within the verified set, were designed but never produced scored evaluations. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\ref{app:negative}).\n172\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.claim_supported]]\n173\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_amip]]\n174\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n175\t\n176\t\\begin{table}[t]\n177\t\\centering\n178\t\\caption{Instrument validation and comparators (sealed evaluation, three seeds). Ratios are mean over the 15 real cases of $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$ with a 95\\% bootstrap CI over cases. Positive values mean the comparator needed more rows.}\n179\t\\label{tab:validity}\n180\t\\footnotesize\n181\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n182\t\\toprule\n183\tCheck & Result & Interpretation \\\\\n184\t\\midrule\n185\tPlanted minimum (synthetic) & $\\kver=8$; score $0$ & equals brute-force minimum \\\\\n186\tRow permutation (Arena) & $\\kver=62$ vs.\\ $62$; score $0$ & invariant to row order \\\\\n187\tSeed agreement (101/102/103) & 15/15 real cases identical & deterministic \\\\\n188\tReplay of one episode & match $=1.0$ & identical output hash \\\\\n189\tFailed episodes & fraction $0.0$ & every proposal verified \\\\\n190\t\\midrule\n191\tvs.\\ random subsets & $3.975$ [$3.934$, $4.0$] & $\\approx 15.7\\times$ fewer rows \\\\\n192\tvs.\\ one-step influence ordering & $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] & identical; no superiority \\\\\n193\tvs.\\ direct heuristic (family scores) & Arena $0$, MTB-GPT-4 $0$, NBA $0$ & never worse \\\\\n194\t & MTB-expert $-0.0053$ & heuristic smaller in $\\ge$1 case \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n198\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#controls]]\n199\t\\end{table}\n200\t\n201\t\\subsection{Tens of comparisons change the leader in every design}\n202\t\\label{sec:res-main}\n203\t\n204\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n205\t\\centering\n206\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fragility_by_design_two_scales.pdf}\n207\t\\caption{Verified perturbation sizes for all 15 real cases, on two scales (log axes). (a) Count $\\kver$ of comparisons dropped or flipped; integers are annotated. (b) The same sizes as a percentage of the source's comparisons, $\\kver/n$. Colors mark the target and perturbation; top-$k$ is top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench (MTB). Sizes are identical across the three seeds.}\n208\t\\label{fig:main}\n209\t\\end{figure}\n210\t\n211\tFigure~\\ref{fig:main} shows the main result. The leader changes after dropping 62 Arena votes, 92 expert judgments, 143 GPT-4 judgments, 7 games of the 2014 NBA season, or 16 games of the 2015 season. Flipping requires 46, 46, 61, 4, and 8 rows, respectively. As fractions, the drop sizes are 0.046\\% of Arena, 2.74\\% of MT-Bench expert judgments, 5.96\\% of GPT-4 judgments, and 0.53\\% and 1.22\\% of the two NBA seasons. The NBA fractions average 0.876\\%.\n212\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n213\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_drop_frac]]\n214\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_top1_drop_frac]]\n215\t\n216\tTwo readings follow. First, \\emph{no design is robust in absolute terms.} The largest top-1 set we needed is 143 comparisons, and the smallest is 4 flipped games. For LLM evaluation, these counts are within reach of a single motivated actor casting votes or of a modest annotation error. Second, \\emph{the 135,622-vote Arena leader is not protected by its data volume.} Its 62-vote certificate is a smaller share of the data than any other leader's by more than an order of magnitude. Scale buys precision for the average contrast. It does not protect the specific contrast at the top, where only a small share of rows carries information about the leader and runner-up (Eq.~\\ref{eq:influence}).\n217\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n218\t\n219\t\\subsection{Drop vs.\\ flip, top-1 vs.\\ top-$k$, and the scale reversal}\n220\t\\label{sec:res-contrasts}\n221\t\n222\t\\paragraph{Flipping is always cheaper than dropping.}\n223\tIn all five sources, the verified flip size for top-1 is smaller than the drop size. The ratio of drop to flip size is 1.35 for Arena, 2.0 for MT-Bench experts, 2.34 for the GPT-4 judge, 1.75 for NBA 2014, and 2.0 for NBA 2015. This matches the first-order prediction of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:influence}. Reversing a verdict both removes evidence for the leader and adds evidence against it, so its effect exceeds that of a drop by the factor $1/(1-p_r)$, which is near 2 for close match-ups. Arena's ratio, the furthest from 2, is not explained by our data. Ties can be dropped but not flipped, and the approximation ignores refitting, so we do not over-interpret it. For manipulation threat models, the flip size is the relevant one: an actor who controls a vote controls its direction.\n224\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#drop_over_flip_top1]]\n225\t\n226\t\\paragraph{The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper, or dearer, than the leader.}\n227\tMoving the top-5 set needs 21 Arena votes, against 62 for the leader. In \\emph{both} NBA seasons, a single game suffices. In MT-Bench the top-3 set costs more than the leader: 229 vs.\\ 92 expert judgments and 150 vs.\\ 143 GPT-4 judgments. The cost is governed by the gap at the boundary being tested, not by the leader's lead. In the NBA seasons, the 5th and 6th teams are close enough that one game reorders them. The evaluator's degenerate-margin check rules out exact ties ($m\\le 10\\tau$). So ``is the top-$k$ stable?'' and ``is the leader stable?'' are different questions, and a leaderboard can pass one and fail the other.\n228\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#topk_over_top1_drop]]\n229\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35e6a956-3122-4827-a187-45bf94db5b6c","uuid":"3ed7725d-c776-4373-abbd-d34f0627ad7b","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:33:56.075Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\title{How Many Comparisons Change the Leader? Refit-Verified Influence Perturbations of Bradley--Terry Leaderboards Across Evaluation Designs}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLeaderboards for language models and sports rank participants by fitting a Bradley--Terry (BT) model to pairwise comparisons. It is rarely measured how much of the comparison data a reported ranking depends on. We measure the smallest perturbation we can find and certify that changes the leader or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard. A perturbation either drops comparisons or reverses their outcomes. The search orders comparisons by their first-order influence on the decisive score margin, and an exact refit verifies every reported size. We evaluate 15 cases spanning crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena), expert and GPT-4-judge annotations (MT-Bench), and two NBA seasons. In every design, tens of comparisons change the leader: 62 of 135,622 Arena votes (0.046\\%), 92 expert and 143 GPT-4 judgments, and 7 and 16 NBA games. These sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations, and identical across seeds. The most fragile design depends on the scale and the target. As a fraction of the data Arena is the most fragile; by count, NBA is. Moving a top-$k$ boundary can cost one game, or more than changing the leader. All sizes are upper bounds; cross-design differences are descriptive, not causal.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nPairwise-comparison leaderboards have become a central instrument of model evaluation. Chatbot Arena aggregates crowdsourced votes between two anonymous chatbots~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}. MT-Bench collects expert and GPT-4 judgments on a fixed question set~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}. Sports ratings descend from the same paired-comparison tradition~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. In each case a Bradley--Terry (BT) model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} turns the comparisons into scores, and the scores into a ranking. Rankings are then used to choose models, to advertise releases, and to compare research systems. Their confidence intervals usually come from resampling or asymptotic arguments~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA,Gao2021UncertaintyQI}. These describe \\emph{random} variation, not how much \\emph{specific} data the conclusion rests on. Recent work shows that crowdsourced leaderboards can be moved deliberately~\\citep{Min2025ImprovingYM}, that evaluation practice shapes them~\\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and that dropping a small number of preferences can alter top Arena rankings~\\citep{Huang2025DroppingJA}. What remains open is how this sensitivity \\emph{compares across evaluation designs}, and how the answer depends on what is measured.\n\nWe ask: \\emph{how small a verified perturbation of the comparison data changes the top-1 or the top-$k$ set of a ridge BT leaderboard? How does that size differ between crowdsourced votes, expert annotation, an LLM judge, and sports results?} We answer with a measurement instrument, not a new estimator. Following the drop-data robustness metric of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}, we order comparisons by a one-step influence approximation~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP} of their effect on the decisive score margin. We find the shortest prefix of that ordering whose removal (or outcome reversal) changes the target. An exact refit checks every candidate. Each reported size is therefore a certified \\emph{upper bound} on the minimal perturbation, not an approximation.\n\nOur central finding has two parts. First, in every design we study, tens of comparisons are enough to change the leader. The verified sets are about $16\\times$ smaller than random perturbations of the same data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}). Second, the question ``which design is most fragile?'' has no scale-free answer. Measured as a fraction of the data, the crowdsourced Arena leader is the most fragile (0.046\\%) and the GPT-4-judge leader the least (5.96\\%). Measured as a count, the NBA leaders are the most fragile (7 and 16 games). The target matters as much as the scale. In both NBA seasons, a single game changes the top-5 set. In MT-Bench, by contrast, the top-3 set costs more than the leader.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_drop_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A refit-verified, influence-ordered protocol for leaderboard fragility. It covers two perturbation types (dropping and flipping comparisons) and two targets (top-1 and top-$k$ set). It is validated against a planted exact minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, \\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n(ii) A cross-design measurement on held-out snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n(iii) Three regularities with stated limits. Flipping always needs fewer comparisons than dropping. The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper or dearer to move than the leader. Fragility rankings across designs reverse between the count and fraction scales (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n(iv) An explicit negative result: the search procedure we evaluate does not improve on the plain influence ordering. The pre-declared test of that improvement is unsupported, so we make no algorithmic-superiority claim (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Paired-comparison models and ranking.}\nThe BT model~\\citep{Bradley1952RankAO} and its extensions to ties~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET} underlie most comparison leaderboards. Ties are often handled, as here, by counting them as half wins. Maximum-likelihood fitting is classical~\\citep{Hunter2003MMAF}. Spectral and counting alternatives come with sample-complexity guarantees for recovering the top-$K$ items~\\citep{Negahban2012RankCR,Chen2015SpectralMT,Chen2017SPECTRALMA,Shah2015SimpleRA}. Elo ratings play the same role in sports and games~\\citep{Elo1978TheRO}. Uncertainty for BT scores is usually quantified by asymptotic theory~\\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI} or the bootstrap~\\citep{Efron1979BootstrapMA}, and the robustness of Elo-style rankings of language models has itself been examined~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}. These tools answer ``how variable is the ranking under resampling?''. We ask a worst-case, data-specific question: ``how few concrete comparisons does the ranking rest on?''.\n\n\\paragraph{Influence and drop-data robustness.}\nInfluence diagnostics go back to Cook's distance~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI}. They were extended to nonlinear models~\\citep{Monari2000WithdrawingAE} and to modern machine learning~\\citep{Koh2017UnderstandingBP}, though they can be fragile for deep networks~\\citep{Basu2020InfluenceFI}. Infinitesimal-jackknife approximations make leave-out analysis cheap~\\citep{Giordano2018ASA}, and higher-order influence expansions refine it~\\citep{Giordano2019AHS,Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ}. \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} turn this machinery into a robustness metric, the approximate maximum influence perturbation: the smallest fraction of data whose removal can overturn a conclusion. For ordinary least squares in low dimensions, such drop-data conclusions can be audited with provable guarantees~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We adopt the drop-data question, apply it to rank conclusions of a BT fit, add an outcome-reversal perturbation, and certify every size by exact refits rather than reporting the linear approximation.\n\n\\paragraph{Manipulation and validity of LLM leaderboards.}\nChatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. On the estimation side, \\citet{Patil2020RankAF} study ranking recovery under BTL-type models when an adversary corrupts a fraction of the pairwise comparisons, which is the setting our flip perturbation instantiates on fixed real data. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n\n\\section{Problem Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Data and estimator.}\nA case consists of $n$ comparison rows $r=(a_r,b_r,y_r)$ among $p$ players. The outcome is $y_r\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$: a win for $a_r$, a tie, or a win for $b_r$. The leaderboard is the ridge-penalized BT maximum-likelihood estimate\n\\begin{equation}\n\\hat\\theta=\\arg\\max_{\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^p}\\;\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\Big[y_r\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{a_r}-\\theta_{b_r})+(1-y_r)\\log\\sigma(\\theta_{b_r}-\\theta_{a_r})\\Big]-\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\theta\\|_2^2 ,\n\\label{eq:bt}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n\n\\paragraph{Targets.}\nLet $S$ be the original top-$k$ set under $\\hat\\theta$. The \\emph{top-$k$ target changes} after a perturbation if the refit $\\tilde\\theta$ satisfies $\\max_{j\\notin S}\\tilde\\theta_j>\\min_{i\\in S}\\tilde\\theta_i+\\tau$, with $\\tau=10^{-6}$. Top-1 is the special case $k=1$: some other player overtakes the leader. We study top-1 for every source, plus top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench, which has few players.\n\n\\paragraph{Perturbations.}\nA \\emph{drop} perturbation deletes a set $D$ of rows. A \\emph{flip} perturbation reverses the outcomes of a set $F$ of decided rows ($y\\mapsto 1-y$). Ties cannot be flipped. Drops model missing or filtered data and targeted removal. Flips model mislabeled or adversarially cast votes: the data volume stays the same and only the verdicts change. The quantity of interest is the minimal size $k^\\star$ of a set that changes the target. We report $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$, the size of a set we construct and verify.\n\n\\paragraph{Two size scales.}\nWe report every size both as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$, and we argue that neither alone is adequate. The fraction is the natural robustness scale of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}. It answers ``what share of the evidence must be wrong?'', which is the relevant question for random noise and data-quality concerns. The count answers ``how many comparisons must an adversary control?'', which is the relevant question for manipulation, where cost scales with votes, not with shares. The two scales can order designs differently when $n$ varies by two orders of magnitude, as it does here. We treat that disagreement as a finding, not a nuisance (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n\n\\section{Method: Refit-Verified Influence Ordering}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\paragraph{Influence of one row on the decisive margin.}\nLet $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin are therefore\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta^{\\text{drop}}_r=-(y_r-p_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}),\\qquad\n\\Delta^{\\text{flip}}_r=(1-2y_r)(v_{a_r}-v_{b_r}).\n\\label{eq:influence}\n\\end{equation}\nRows are sorted by ascending $\\Delta_r$, so the rows that shrink the margin most come first. One linear solve gives $v$; the ordering costs $O(p^3+n\\log n)$. Equation~\\eqref{eq:influence} also predicts two qualitative regularities that we test in \\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}. First, for a row won by $a_r$ ($y_r=1$), the flip effect exceeds the drop effect by the factor $1/(1-p_r)\\ge 1$, which equals 2 for an even match-up. Flips should therefore need fewer rows. Second, $|v_{a}-v_{b}|$ shrinks as the information about the $L$--$C$ contrast grows. The \\emph{count} needed should thus scale with the margin times that information, while the \\emph{fraction} also falls when the rows touching $L$ and $C$ are a small share of a large data set.\n\n\\paragraph{Exact verification by prefix search.}\nThe linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$, and the target condition is tested on the result. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is a single NumPy module (Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\n\n\\paragraph{What a verified size means.}\n$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings such as low-dimensional least squares~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. Even there, exact search and certificates that no smaller set exists become computationally demanding beyond a few dimensions, while greedy and influence-based heuristics only exhibit sets and therefore give upper bounds~\\citep{Freund2023TowardsPR}. We do not claim to solve the problem on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n\n\\paragraph{Comparators.}\nTwo comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity controls.}\n\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\n\n\\paragraph{Data and protocol.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams, following the long use of BT models for sports tournaments~\\citep{Cattelan2013DynamicBM}. We fit a static BT model to each season, without the time-varying strengths such models can include. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evaluation designs and cases. $n$ is the number of comparison rows after filtering, recovered exactly from the evaluator's reported fraction $\\kver/n$ and the verified size $\\kver$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}). Every source contributes drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$ cases.}\n\\label{tab:cases}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrcl@{}}\n\\toprule\nDesign & Source (pinned snapshot) & $n$ & top-$k$ & What a row is \\\\\n\\midrule\nCrowdsourced votes & Chatbot Arena human pref.\\ (140k) & 135{,}622 & 5 & one user vote \\\\\nExpert annotation & MT-Bench human judgments & 3{,}355 & 3 & one expert judgment \\\\\nLLM judge & MT-Bench GPT-4 pair judgments & 2{,}400 & 3 & one GPT-4 verdict \\\\\nSports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\nSports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n\\midrule\nControl & planted 3-player case (synthetic) & -- & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\nControl & Arena drop/top-1 with rows permuted & 135{,}622 & -- & drop/top-1 only \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#n_rows_derived]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\n\\label{sec:res-validity}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:validity} collects the evidence that the measured sizes mean what we claim. On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. This score is $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$, so a value of 0 means the instrument returned exactly the brute-force minimum. Here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.synthetic_control_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.permutation_control_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_match]]\n\nAgainst the random comparator, the influence-ordered sets are far smaller. The mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{rand}}/\\kver)$ is 3.975, with 95\\% CI [3.934, 4.0]. This corresponds to a geometric-mean factor of about $15.7\\times$. The upper end of the interval sits at the clip value of 4, so most cases are censored, and the true advantage is \\emph{at least} this large. Fragility to chosen perturbations is therefore a different quantity from fragility to noise. A leader that survives typical random corruption of tens of comparisons can still fall to a targeted set of the same size.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_random]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#random_ratio_x]]\n\nAgainst the direct heuristic, the influence ordering was never worse in the Arena, GPT-4-judge, and NBA families: each family score is 0. In the MT-Bench-expert family, the score of $-0.0053$ shows that the heuristic was marginally smaller in at least one case. The evaluator records this only at family level. The ordering therefore improves on the obvious ``remove the leader's wins'' attack in most cases, but not uniformly.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n\n\\paragraph{No improvement over the plain influence ordering.}\nThe configuration evaluated here \\emph{is} the influence ordering plus verified prefix search. Its sizes coincide with those of the evaluator's own one-step-influence comparator in every case: the mean $\\log_2$ ratio is 0.0, with CI [0.0, 0.0]. The top-1 drop fractions are equal to the reported precision. The pre-declared test of a strict improvement over this ordering (CI lower bound $>0$) is therefore \\emph{not supported}. We make no claim of algorithmic superiority. Refinements that relinearize the influence after partial removals, or that prune and swap rows within the verified set, were designed but never produced scored evaluations. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\ref{app:negative}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.claim_supported]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mean_log2_ratio_vs_amip]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Instrument validation and comparators (sealed evaluation, three seeds). Ratios are mean over the 15 real cases of $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$ with a 95\\% bootstrap CI over cases. Positive values mean the comparator needed more rows.}\n\\label{tab:validity}\n\\footnotesize\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n\\toprule\nCheck & Result & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nPlanted minimum (synthetic) & $\\kver=8$; score $0$ & equals brute-force minimum \\\\\nRow permutation (Arena) & $\\kver=62$ vs.\\ $62$; score $0$ & invariant to row order \\\\\nSeed agreement (101/102/103) & 15/15 real cases identical & deterministic \\\\\nReplay of one episode & match $=1.0$ & identical output hash \\\\\nFailed episodes & fraction $0.0$ & every proposal verified \\\\\n\\midrule\nvs.\\ random subsets & $3.975$ [$3.934$, $4.0$] & $\\approx 15.7\\times$ fewer rows \\\\\nvs.\\ one-step influence ordering & $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] & identical; no superiority \\\\\nvs.\\ direct heuristic (family scores) & Arena $0$, MTB-GPT-4 $0$, NBA $0$ & never worse \\\\\n & MTB-expert $-0.0053$ & heuristic smaller in $\\ge$1 case \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#controls]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Tens of comparisons change the leader in every design}\n\\label{sec:res-main}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fragility_by_design_two_scales.pdf}\n\\caption{Verified perturbation sizes for all 15 real cases, on two scales (log axes). (a) Count $\\kver$ of comparisons dropped or flipped; integers are annotated. (b) The same sizes as a percentage of the source's comparisons, $\\kver/n$. Colors mark the target and perturbation; top-$k$ is top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench (MTB). Sizes are identical across the three seeds.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\n\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main} shows the main result. The leader changes after dropping 62 Arena votes, 92 expert judgments, 143 GPT-4 judgments, 7 games of the 2014 NBA season, or 16 games of the 2015 season. Flipping requires 46, 46, 61, 4, and 8 rows, respectively. As fractions, the drop sizes are 0.046\\% of Arena, 2.74\\% of MT-Bench expert judgments, 5.96\\% of GPT-4 judgments, and 0.53\\% and 1.22\\% of the two NBA seasons. The NBA fractions average 0.876\\%.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_drop_frac]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_top1_drop_frac]]\n\nTwo readings follow. First, \\emph{no design is robust in absolute terms.} The largest top-1 set we needed is 143 comparisons, and the smallest is 4 flipped games. For LLM evaluation, these counts are within reach of a single motivated actor casting votes or of a modest annotation error. Second, \\emph{the 135,622-vote Arena leader is not protected by its data volume.} Its 62-vote certificate is a smaller share of the data than any other leader's by more than an order of magnitude. Scale buys precision for the average contrast. It does not protect the specific contrast at the top, where only a small share of rows carries information about the leader and runner-up (Eq.~\\ref{eq:influence}).\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_top1_drop_frac]]\n\n\\subsection{Drop vs.\\ flip, top-1 vs.\\ top-$k$, and the scale reversal}\n\\label{sec:res-contrasts}\n\n\\paragraph{Flipping is always cheaper than dropping.}\nIn all five sources, the verified flip size for top-1 is smaller than the drop size. The ratio of drop to flip size is 1.35 for Arena, 2.0 for MT-Bench experts, 2.34 for the GPT-4 judge, 1.75 for NBA 2014, and 2.0 for NBA 2015. This matches the first-order prediction of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:influence}. Reversing a verdict both removes evidence for the leader and adds evidence against it, so its effect exceeds that of a drop by the factor $1/(1-p_r)$, which is near 2 for close match-ups. Arena's ratio, the furthest from 2, is not explained by our data. Ties can be dropped but not flipped, and the approximation ignores refitting, so we do not over-interpret it. For manipulation threat models, the flip size is the relevant one: an actor who controls a vote controls its direction.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#drop_over_flip_top1]]\n\n\\paragraph{The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper, or dearer, than the leader.}\nMoving the top-5 set needs 21 Arena votes, against 62 for the leader. In \\emph{both} NBA seasons, a single game suffices. In MT-Bench the top-3 set costs more than the leader: 229 vs.\\ 92 expert judgments and 150 vs.\\ 143 GPT-4 judgments. The cost is governed by the gap at the boundary being tested, not by the leader's lead. In the NBA seasons, the 5th and 6th teams are close enough that one game reorders them. The evaluator's degenerate-margin check rules out exact ties ($m\\le 10\\tau$). So ``is the top-$k$ stable?'' and ``is the leader stable?'' are different questions, and a leaderboard can pass one and fail the other.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#topk_over_top1_drop]]\n","numLines":200,"startLine":30,"totalLines":377}}}
