{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BWv47Y6yF2mCFZ1moees6v","type":"tool_result","content":"229\t\n230\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n231\t\\centering\n232\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/controlled_contrasts.pdf}\n233\t\\caption{Controlled contrasts. (a) MT-Bench: the same question set judged by expert humans (filled circles) and by GPT-4 (open squares). Values are $\\kver/n$ in percent, with counts annotated. The GPT-4 leader needs a larger share of its data to change for top-1, but a smaller one for the top-3 set. (b) Fragility rank of the drop/top-1 cases by count (left, with $\\kver$) and by fraction (right, with $\\kver/n$). Rank 1 (top) is the most fragile. The ordering of Arena and the NBA seasons reverses between scales (purple).}\n234\t\\label{fig:contrasts}\n235\t\\end{figure}\n236\t\n237\t\\paragraph{Expert humans vs.\\ an LLM judge on the same questions.}\n238\tMT-Bench is the closest thing to a controlled comparison in our data: the same benchmark is judged by experts and by GPT-4 (Fig.~\\ref{fig:contrasts}a). For top-1, the GPT-4-judge leader is harder to dislodge, at 143 vs.\\ 92 drops (5.96\\% vs.\\ 2.74\\%) and 61 vs.\\ 46 flips. For the top-3 set the order reverses: 150 vs.\\ 229 drops (6.25\\% vs.\\ 6.83\\%). Neither annotation source is uniformly more robust. The two sources also differ in $n$ (2,400 vs.\\ 3,355), in tie rates, and possibly in which model leads, since player identities are hidden from the finder. The contrast is therefore not a causal estimate of a ``judge effect''.\n239\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n240\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_flip_frac]]\n241\t\n242\t\\paragraph{Fragility rankings reverse between scales.}\n243\tFigure~\\ref{fig:contrasts}b orders the five drop/top-1 cases by count and by fraction. By count, the two NBA seasons are the most fragile (7, 16), followed by Arena (62). By fraction, Arena moves to first place (0.0457\\%), ahead of NBA 2014 (0.531\\%) and NBA 2015 (1.22\\%). The two MT-Bench sources stay last on both scales. A cross-benchmark statement such as ``crowdsourced leaderboards are more fragile than sports tables'' is therefore true on one natural scale and false on the other. Any such comparison must name its scale and justify it by the threat model (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}).\n244\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n245\t\n246\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n247\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n248\t\n249\t\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.}\n250\tFor one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n251\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n252\t\n253\t\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\n254\tEvery size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Certifying lower bounds is hard even for least squares in a few dimensions~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO,Freund2023TowardsPR}, and a BT fit with tens of players is well outside that regime. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n255\t\n256\t\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty on $\\kver$.}\n257\tThe three seeds give identical sizes because the finder is deterministic. They do not quantify how $\\kver$ would vary under resampling of comparisons or under a different data snapshot. The bootstrap intervals in Table~\\ref{tab:validity} are over cases, for comparator ratios, not over data. The random-comparator summary is censored at the clip of $\\log_2=4$.\n258\t\n259\t\\paragraph{One estimator, one snapshot per source.}\n260\tAll results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design replication across releases.\n261\t\n262\t\\paragraph{Confounded cross-design differences.}\n263\tThe designs differ in $n$, number of players, comparison-graph structure, tie rate, and the size of the decisive margin. With one case per design and target, we cannot attribute differences to the design itself, such as crowd versus judge. The cross-design statements are descriptive.\n264\t\n265\t\\paragraph{Comparator and search coverage.}\n266\tThe direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Iterative relinearization and set-refinement variants were not evaluated, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n267\t\n268\t\\section{Conclusion}\n269\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n270\t\n271\tWe measured how many comparisons must be dropped or reversed to change a Bradley--Terry leaderboard. The instrument orders comparisons by their influence on the decisive margin and certifies each size by exact refits; on a planted case it recovers the true minimum. Across crowdsourced votes, expert and LLM-judge annotations, and professional sports, tens of comparisons suffice to change the leader. These sets are roughly sixteen times smaller than random perturbations: fragility to chosen data differs from fragility to noise. Cross-design comparisons are scale- and target-dependent. The Arena leader is the most fragile as a share of its data but not as a count. NBA top-5 sets move with one game. MT-Bench top-3 sets resist more than their leaders. Expert and GPT-4 judges trade places between targets. We therefore recommend reporting verified fragility on both scales and for each advertised target. Such reports should be read as upper bounds on a fixed estimator, not as minima or causal properties of an evaluation design. Tighter certificates, data-resampling uncertainty for $\\kver$, and controlled within-design replications are the natural next steps.\n272\t\n273\t% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n274\t% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n275\t\\clearpage\n276\t\\bibliography{references}\n277\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n278\t\n279\t\\clearpage\n280\t\\appendix\n281\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n282\t\\label{app:si}\n283\t\n284\t\\subsection{Per-case verified sizes}\n285\t\\label{app:percase}\n286\t\n287\tTable~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\n288\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n289\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n290\t\n291\t\\begin{table}[h]\n292\t\\centering\n293\t\\caption{Verified sizes per case (identical for seeds 101/102/103). $n$ is derived as in Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}. Percentages are $100\\,\\kver/n$.}\n294\t\\label{tab:percase}\n295\t\\small\n296\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrr@{}}\n297\t\\toprule\n298\tSource & Case & $\\kver$ & $n$ & $\\kver/n$ (\\%) \\\\\n299\t\\midrule\n300\tArena (crowd) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n301\t & flip, top-1 & 46 & 135{,}622 & 0.0339 \\\\\n302\t & drop, top-5 set & 21 & 135{,}622 & 0.0155 \\\\\n303\tMT-Bench (expert) & drop, top-1 & 92 & 3{,}355 & 2.74 \\\\\n304\t & flip, top-1 & 46 & 3{,}355 & 1.37 \\\\\n305\t & drop, top-3 set & 229 & 3{,}355 & 6.83 \\\\\n306\tMT-Bench (GPT-4 judge) & drop, top-1 & 143 & 2{,}400 & 5.96 \\\\\n307\t & flip, top-1 & 61 & 2{,}400 & 2.54 \\\\\n308\t & drop, top-3 set & 150 & 2{,}400 & 6.25 \\\\\n309\tNBA 2014 & drop, top-1 & 7 & 1{,}319 & 0.531 \\\\\n310\t & flip, top-1 & 4 & 1{,}319 & 0.303 \\\\\n311\t & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}319 & 0.0758 \\\\\n312\tNBA 2015 & drop, top-1 & 16 & 1{,}311 & 1.22 \\\\\n313\t & flip, top-1 & 8 & 1{,}311 & 0.610 \\\\\n314\t & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}311 & 0.0763 \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tSynthetic control & drop, top-1 & 8 & -- & -- \\\\\n317\tPermutation control (Arena) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n321\t\\end{table}\n322\t\n323\t\\subsection{Recovering $n$ from the evaluation record}\n324\t\\label{app:derivation}\n325\t\n326\tThe evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}.\n327\t% @evidence[[analysis/derive_stats.py]]\n328\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_flip_frac]]\n329\t\n330\t\\subsection{Estimator, cases, comparators and controls in detail}\n331\t\\label{app:details}\n332\t\n333\t\\paragraph{Case construction.}\n334\tFor each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n335\t\n336\t\\paragraph{Random comparator.}\n337\tFor a candidate size $s$, 20 uniformly random subsets of size $s$ are drawn from the perturbable rows, with early stopping. The size counts as changing the target if at least 10 of them change it. The smallest such $s$ is found by doubling and bisection up to half of the perturbable rows. Failure to find one is recorded as $n+1$ (censored). Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream (lines 611--626).\n338\t\n339\t\\paragraph{Direct heuristic.}\n340\tRows where the leader beat the challenger come first, in index order. Then come the leader's other wins, sorted by decreasing fitted strength of the beaten opponent. The same verified prefix search is applied (lines 595--608).\n341\t\n342\t\\paragraph{Planted control.}\n343\tThree players meet in three pairs of 50--80 games each. One pair is nearly balanced, with one to three wins above an even split; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70. Before the case is accepted, its exact minimal drop size is found by enumerating all compositions of per-pair winner classes up to size 8. Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected (lines 658--721). The finder returned a size-8 set, equal to the enumerated minimum.\n344\t\n345\t\\paragraph{Scoring.}\n346\tFor real cases, each seed's score is $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(\\min(k_{\\text{infl}},k_{\\text{heur}})/\\kver),-4,4)$. Here $k_{\\text{infl}}$ is the evaluator's one-step-influence comparator, and an unverified proposal scores $-4$. Family scores are weighted means over the family's cases. Because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\text{infl}}$, a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller. The only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only evidence that the heuristic was smaller anywhere.\n347\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n348\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_score]]\n349\t\n350\t\\subsection{Pre-declared superiority test and unevaluated refinements}\n351\t\\label{app:negative}\n352\t\n353\tThe study pre-declared one inferential claim: the evaluated search would find strictly smaller verified sets than the one-step influence ordering. Specifically, the bootstrap lower bound of the mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{infl}}/\\kver)$ would have to exceed 0. The selected configuration is the plain influence ordering with verified prefix search, so this ratio is 0.0 with CI [0.0, 0.0], and the claim is not supported. The evaluation still completed and qualified on every validity gate. The sizes in this paper are therefore valid measurements, and the negative outcome concerns only algorithmic improvement.\n354\t\n355\tThree refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. All three relinearize the influence after partial removals and exploit the fact that \\eqref{eq:bt} depends only on per-pair counts. The first adds multi-pair targeting and exact pruning. The second adds deterministic refit budgets and a two-for-one swap refinement. The third adds exact pruning seeded from the influence set. None produced a scored evaluation: all three of three were unscored because execution failed before evaluation. Their effect on $\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss. No result in the main text depends on them.\n356\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n357\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\n358\t\n359\t\\subsection{Reproducibility record}\n360\t\\label{app:repro}\n361\t\n362\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n363\tAll data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n364\t\n365\t\\paragraph{Software and compute.}\n366\tPython with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\nolinkurl{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n367\t\n368\t\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\n369\tPerturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree); BT refit at lines 72--117, influence ordering and verified prefix search at lines 151--217. Evaluator: \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}. Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.\n370\t\n371\t\\subsection{LLM usage}\n372\t\\label{app:llm}\n373\t\n374\tLarge language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.\n375\t\n376\t\\end{document}\n377\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35e6a956-3122-4827-a187-45bf94db5b6c","uuid":"af929341-122b-4eeb-a8e4-d957b737673e","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:33:58.063Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/controlled_contrasts.pdf}\n\\caption{Controlled contrasts. (a) MT-Bench: the same question set judged by expert humans (filled circles) and by GPT-4 (open squares). Values are $\\kver/n$ in percent, with counts annotated. The GPT-4 leader needs a larger share of its data to change for top-1, but a smaller one for the top-3 set. (b) Fragility rank of the drop/top-1 cases by count (left, with $\\kver$) and by fraction (right, with $\\kver/n$). Rank 1 (top) is the most fragile. The ordering of Arena and the NBA seasons reverses between scales (purple).}\n\\label{fig:contrasts}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{Expert humans vs.\\ an LLM judge on the same questions.}\nMT-Bench is the closest thing to a controlled comparison in our data: the same benchmark is judged by experts and by GPT-4 (Fig.~\\ref{fig:contrasts}a). For top-1, the GPT-4-judge leader is harder to dislodge, at 143 vs.\\ 92 drops (5.96\\% vs.\\ 2.74\\%) and 61 vs.\\ 46 flips. For the top-3 set the order reverses: 150 vs.\\ 229 drops (6.25\\% vs.\\ 6.83\\%). Neither annotation source is uniformly more robust. The two sources also differ in $n$ (2,400 vs.\\ 3,355), in tie rates, and possibly in which model leads, since player identities are hidden from the finder. The contrast is therefore not a causal estimate of a ``judge effect''.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Fragility rankings reverse between scales.}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:contrasts}b orders the five drop/top-1 cases by count and by fraction. By count, the two NBA seasons are the most fragile (7, 16), followed by Arena (62). By fraction, Arena moves to first place (0.0457\\%), ahead of NBA 2014 (0.531\\%) and NBA 2015 (1.22\\%). The two MT-Bench sources stay last on both scales. A cross-benchmark statement such as ``crowdsourced leaderboards are more fragile than sports tables'' is therefore true on one natural scale and false on the other. Any such comparison must name its scale and justify it by the threat model (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}).\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.}\nFor one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\nEvery size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Certifying lower bounds is hard even for least squares in a few dimensions~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO,Freund2023TowardsPR}, and a BT fit with tens of players is well outside that regime. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n\n\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty on $\\kver$.}\nThe three seeds give identical sizes because the finder is deterministic. They do not quantify how $\\kver$ would vary under resampling of comparisons or under a different data snapshot. The bootstrap intervals in Table~\\ref{tab:validity} are over cases, for comparator ratios, not over data. The random-comparator summary is censored at the clip of $\\log_2=4$.\n\n\\paragraph{One estimator, one snapshot per source.}\nAll results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design replication across releases.\n\n\\paragraph{Confounded cross-design differences.}\nThe designs differ in $n$, number of players, comparison-graph structure, tie rate, and the size of the decisive margin. With one case per design and target, we cannot attribute differences to the design itself, such as crowd versus judge. The cross-design statements are descriptive.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparator and search coverage.}\nThe direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Iterative relinearization and set-refinement variants were not evaluated, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measured how many comparisons must be dropped or reversed to change a Bradley--Terry leaderboard. The instrument orders comparisons by their influence on the decisive margin and certifies each size by exact refits; on a planted case it recovers the true minimum. Across crowdsourced votes, expert and LLM-judge annotations, and professional sports, tens of comparisons suffice to change the leader. These sets are roughly sixteen times smaller than random perturbations: fragility to chosen data differs from fragility to noise. Cross-design comparisons are scale- and target-dependent. The Arena leader is the most fragile as a share of its data but not as a count. NBA top-5 sets move with one game. MT-Bench top-3 sets resist more than their leaders. Expert and GPT-4 judges trade places between targets. We therefore recommend reporting verified fragility on both scales and for each advertised target. Such reports should be read as upper bounds on a fixed estimator, not as minima or causal properties of an evaluation design. Tighter certificates, data-resampling uncertainty for $\\kver$, and controlled within-design replications are the natural next steps.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\label{app:si}\n\n\\subsection{Per-case verified sizes}\n\\label{app:percase}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Verified sizes per case (identical for seeds 101/102/103). $n$ is derived as in Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}. Percentages are $100\\,\\kver/n$.}\n\\label{tab:percase}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrr@{}}\n\\toprule\nSource & Case & $\\kver$ & $n$ & $\\kver/n$ (\\%) \\\\\n\\midrule\nArena (crowd) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 46 & 135{,}622 & 0.0339 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 21 & 135{,}622 & 0.0155 \\\\\nMT-Bench (expert) & drop, top-1 & 92 & 3{,}355 & 2.74 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 46 & 3{,}355 & 1.37 \\\\\n & drop, top-3 set & 229 & 3{,}355 & 6.83 \\\\\nMT-Bench (GPT-4 judge) & drop, top-1 & 143 & 2{,}400 & 5.96 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 61 & 2{,}400 & 2.54 \\\\\n & drop, top-3 set & 150 & 2{,}400 & 6.25 \\\\\nNBA 2014 & drop, top-1 & 7 & 1{,}319 & 0.531 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 4 & 1{,}319 & 0.303 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}319 & 0.0758 \\\\\nNBA 2015 & drop, top-1 & 16 & 1{,}311 & 1.22 \\\\\n & flip, top-1 & 8 & 1{,}311 & 0.610 \\\\\n & drop, top-5 set & 1 & 1{,}311 & 0.0763 \\\\\n\\midrule\nSynthetic control & drop, top-1 & 8 & -- & -- \\\\\nPermutation control (Arena) & drop, top-1 & 62 & 135{,}622 & 0.0457 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Recovering $n$ from the evaluation record}\n\\label{app:derivation}\n\nThe evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derive_stats.py]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.nba_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\subsection{Estimator, cases, comparators and controls in detail}\n\\label{app:details}\n\n\\paragraph{Case construction.}\nFor each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n\n\\paragraph{Random comparator.}\nFor a candidate size $s$, 20 uniformly random subsets of size $s$ are drawn from the perturbable rows, with early stopping. The size counts as changing the target if at least 10 of them change it. The smallest such $s$ is found by doubling and bisection up to half of the perturbable rows. Failure to find one is recorded as $n+1$ (censored). Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream (lines 611--626).\n\n\\paragraph{Direct heuristic.}\nRows where the leader beat the challenger come first, in index order. Then come the leader's other wins, sorted by decreasing fitted strength of the beaten opponent. The same verified prefix search is applied (lines 595--608).\n\n\\paragraph{Planted control.}\nThree players meet in three pairs of 50--80 games each. One pair is nearly balanced, with one to three wins above an even split; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70. Before the case is accepted, its exact minimal drop size is found by enumerating all compositions of per-pair winner classes up to size 8. Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected (lines 658--721). The finder returned a size-8 set, equal to the enumerated minimum.\n\n\\paragraph{Scoring.}\nFor real cases, each seed's score is $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(\\min(k_{\\text{infl}},k_{\\text{heur}})/\\kver),-4,4)$. Here $k_{\\text{infl}}$ is the evaluator's one-step-influence comparator, and an unverified proposal scores $-4$. Family scores are weighted means over the family's cases. Because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\text{infl}}$, a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller. The only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only evidence that the heuristic was smaller anywhere.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_human_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.arena140k_score]]\n\n\\subsection{Pre-declared superiority test and unevaluated refinements}\n\\label{app:negative}\n\nThe study pre-declared one inferential claim: the evaluated search would find strictly smaller verified sets than the one-step influence ordering. Specifically, the bootstrap lower bound of the mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{infl}}/\\kver)$ would have to exceed 0. The selected configuration is the plain influence ordering with verified prefix search, so this ratio is 0.0 with CI [0.0, 0.0], and the claim is not supported. The evaluation still completed and qualified on every validity gate. The sizes in this paper are therefore valid measurements, and the negative outcome concerns only algorithmic improvement.\n\nThree refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. All three relinearize the influence after partial removals and exploit the fact that \\eqref{eq:bt} depends only on per-pair counts. The first adds multi-pair targeting and exact pruning. The second adds deterministic refit budgets and a two-for-one swap refinement. The third adds exact pruning seeded from the influence set. None produced a scored evaluation: all three of three were unscored because execution failed before evaluation. Their effect on $\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss. No result in the main text depends on them.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility record}\n\\label{app:repro}\n\n\\paragraph{Data.}\nAll data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n\n\\paragraph{Software and compute.}\nPython with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\nolinkurl{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n\n\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\nPerturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree); BT refit at lines 72--117, influence ordering and verified prefix search at lines 151--217. Evaluator: \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}. Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.\n\n\\subsection{LLM usage}\n\\label{app:llm}\n\nLarge language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":149,"startLine":229,"totalLines":377}}}
