{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVb92wxS78NbvH91RvxZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018tDD3JG1c9DJmPwsBAXzWn","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cp paper.tex /tmp/paper.tex.bak_round1; cp claims_grounding.json /tmp/claims.bak; cp paper_strategy.json /tmp/strategy.bak; cp figure_manifest.json /tmp/manifest.bak; grep -n \"evaluate_final\\|split_manifest\\|requirements.txt\\|held-out\\|lines [0-9]\" paper.tex","description":"Back up files and locate evaluator citations"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1228,"cache_read_input_tokens":92904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1228},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35e6a956-3122-4827-a187-45bf94db5b6c","uuid":"d608a865-277d-4103-a94b-74376bfe73bc","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:34:34.899Z","request_id":"req_011CgVb91qyfSeSP7dYEWHLf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018tDD3JG1c9DJmPwsBAXzWn","type":"tool_result","content":"58:(ii) A cross-design measurement on held-out snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n120:Two comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n123:\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\n334:For each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n337:For a candidate size $s$, 20 uniformly random subsets of size $s$ are drawn from the perturbable rows, with early stopping. The size counts as changing the target if at least 10 of them change it. The smallest such $s$ is found by doubling and bisection up to half of the perturbable rows. Failure to find one is recorded as $n+1$ (censored). Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream (lines 611--626).\n340:Rows where the leader beat the challenger come first, in index order. Then come the leader's other wins, sorted by decreasing fitted strength of the beaten opponent. The same verified prefix search is applied (lines 595--608).\n343:Three players meet in three pairs of 50--80 games each. One pair is nearly balanced, with one to three wins above an even split; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70. Before the case is accepted, its exact minimal drop size is found by enumerating all compositions of per-pair winner classes up to size 8. Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected (lines 658--721). The finder returned a size-8 set, equal to the enumerated minimum.\n363:All data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n366:Python with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\nolinkurl{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n369:Perturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree); BT refit at lines 72--117, influence ordering and verified prefix search at lines 151--217. Evaluator: \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}. Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35e6a956-3122-4827-a187-45bf94db5b6c","uuid":"ce1e082b-43ec-41e3-a94a-e401f765f19a","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:34:35.011Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"58:(ii) A cross-design measurement on held-out snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n120:Two comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n123:\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\n334:For each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n337:For a candidate size $s$, 20 uniformly random subsets of size $s$ are drawn from the perturbable rows, with early stopping. The size counts as changing the target if at least 10 of them change it. The smallest such $s$ is found by doubling and bisection up to half of the perturbable rows. Failure to find one is recorded as $n+1$ (censored). Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream (lines 611--626).\n340:Rows where the leader beat the challenger come first, in index order. Then come the leader's other wins, sorted by decreasing fitted strength of the beaten opponent. The same verified prefix search is applied (lines 595--608).\n343:Three players meet in three pairs of 50--80 games each. One pair