{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVbAkhxxEm1rB5D1VEWF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012rKAUWPxYom1oXysjvbeyb","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cat > /tmp/edit1.py <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('paper.tex'); t=p.read_text()\nR=[]\ndef r(old,new):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(old)==1, (t.count(old), old[:80])\n    t=t.replace(old,new)\n\n# contributions\nr(\"(ii) A cross-design measurement on held-out snapshots of four evaluation designs,\",\n  \"(ii) A cross-design measurement on pinned public snapshots of four evaluation designs,\")\nr(\"It is validated against a planted exact minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay\",\n  \"It is checked against a planted case with an enumerated minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay\")\n\n# Problem setup estimator paragraph\nr(\"where $\\\\sigma$ is the logistic function, ties count as half a win for each side, and $\\\\lambda=0.1$. The ridge term\",\n  \"where $\\\\sigma$ is the logistic function and ties count as half a win for each side. The ridge weight $\\\\lambda$ and the tolerance $\\\\tau$ below are passed to the finder by the evaluation harness with each request; the harness specifies $\\\\lambda=0.1$ and $\\\\tau=10^{-6}$. The ridge term\")\nr(\"Players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. After that the player set is fixed:\",\n  \"According to the harness's stated protocol, players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. The player set is then fixed:\")\nr(\" The estimator constants are fixed in the evaluator (\\\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 129--135), and the finder's own refit mirrors them (source locations in Appendix~\\\\ref{app:repro}).\",\n  \" The finder's refit implements exactly this objective (source locations in Appendix~\\\\ref{app:repro}).\")\nr(\"satisfies $\\\\max_{j\\\\notin S}\\\\tilde\\\\theta_j>\\\\min_{i\\\\in S}\\\\tilde\\\\theta_i+\\\\tau$, with $\\\\tau=10^{-6}$.\",\n  \"satisfies $\\\\max_{j\\\\notin S}\\\\tilde\\\\theta_j>\\\\min_{i\\\\in S}\\\\tilde\\\\theta_i+\\\\tau$.\")\n\n# Provenance paragraph: insert after Two size scales paragraph\nr(\"We treat that disagreement as a finding, not a nuisance (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\\n\",\n  \"We treat that disagreement as a finding, not a nuisance (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{What is inspectable.}\\nThe study has two components. The \\\\emph{finder}, which constructs perturbation sets, is released in full, and every statement we make about it can be checked against its source. The \\\\emph{evaluation harness}, which builds the cases, re-verifies each returned set with its own refit, runs the comparators and controls, and computes all reported scores and fractions, is not part of the artifacts accompanying this paper. Its numerical outputs are our evidence. Its internal settings (the player filter, tie mapping, comparator and control construction, data splitting, and scoring rules) are described below as the harness's \\\\emph{stated protocol}. They could not be checked against source here.\\n\")\n\n# Method: verification paragraph gradient norm\nr(\"warm-started at $\\\\hat\\\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$,\",\n  \"warm-started at $\\\\hat\\\\theta$ and stopped once the largest absolute gradient entry falls below $10^{-10}$,\")\nr(\"and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit.\",\n  \"and the harness re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit.\")\n\n# Eq. 2 predictions: only first tested\nr(\"Equation~\\\\eqref{eq:influence} also predicts two qualitative regularities that we test in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}. First,\",\n  \"Equation~\\\\eqref{eq:influence} also suggests two qualitative regularities. First,\")\nr(\"Flips should therefore need fewer rows. Second, $|v_{a}-v_{b}|$ shrinks as the information about the $L$--$C$ contrast grows. The \\\\emph{count} needed should thus scale with the margin times that information, while the \\\\emph{fraction} also falls when the rows touching $L$ and $C$ are a small share of a large data set.\",\n  \"Flips should therefore need fewer rows; we compare this with the verified sizes in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}. Second, $|v_{a}-v_{b}|$ shrinks as the information about the $L$--$C$ contrast grows. This suggests that the \\\\emph{count} needed scales with the margin times that information, while the \\\\emph{fraction} also falls when the rows touching $L$ and $C$ are a small share of a large data set. We did not measure decisive margins, boundary gaps, or the share of rows involving the decisive pair, so this second regularity is a hypothesis that our data cannot test.\")\n\n# Method: what a verified size means - planted case wording\nr(\"Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\",\n  \"Instead, we check the instrument on a planted case whose minimum the harness computes by enumeration (below).\")\n\n# Comparators\nr(\"Two comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\\\emph{Random:}\",\n  \"The harness runs two comparators on identical data. We describe them according to its stated protocol. \\\\emph{Random:}\")\nr(\" A 95\\\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\",\n  \" The harness reports a 95\\\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases (stated as 2{,}000 resamples).\")\n\n# Validity controls\nr(\"\\\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration\",\n  \"These are also part of the harness's stated protocol. \\\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. The harness computes its exact minimal drop size by brute-force enumeration\")\nr(\"Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\\\le 10\\\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\\\hat\\\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\",\n  \"Before any search, the harness is stated to check each case. It rejects cases with a degenerate decisive margin ($m\\\\le 10\\\\tau$). It also requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged and that a refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\\\hat\\\\theta$.\")\n\n# Data and protocol\nr(\"Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties.\",\n  \"Under the harness's stated protocol, Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties.\")\nr(\"All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once\",\n  \"Method choices were made on separate development data, stated by the harness to be the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. The harness is also stated to exclude final rows whose content hash appears in the development data. Neither the split nor its overlap can be inspected in our artifacts. The 17 cases were then run once\")\nr(\"Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed.\",\n  \"Every episode either returns a set that the harness verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed.\")\np.write_text(t)\nprint('ok')\nEOF\npython3 /tmp/edit1.py","description":"Apply provenance-qualification edits to setup and method"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_se