{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVbCSPSJrk12WUjKPmvD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01664qEdvj7Jd7FmdMEkvmKi","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cat > /tmp/edit2.py <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('paper.tex'); t=p.read_text()\ndef r(old,new):\n    global t\n    assert t.count(old)==1, (t.count(old), old[:80])\n    t=t.replace(old,new)\n\nr(\"\\\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\",\n  \"\\\\subsection{The instrument matches a planted minimum, is order-invariant, and is deterministic}\")\nr(\"On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. This score is $\\\\min(0,\\\\log_2(k^\\\\star/\\\\kver))$, so a value of 0 means the instrument returned exactly the brute-force minimum. Here, at least, the upper bound is tight.\",\n  \"On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. The harness defines this score as $\\\\min(0,\\\\log_2(k^\\\\star/\\\\kver))$ with $k^\\\\star$ its enumerated minimum. Under that stated definition, a value of 0 means the instrument returned the brute-force minimum, so here, at least, the upper bound is tight.\")\nr(\"Against the direct heuristic, the influence ordering was never worse in the Arena, GPT-4-judge, and NBA families: each family score is 0. In the MT-Bench-expert family, the score of $-0.0053$ shows that the heuristic was marginally smaller in at least one case. The evaluator records this only at family level. The ordering therefore improves on the obvious ``remove the leader's wins'' attack in most cases, but not uniformly.\",\n  \"The direct heuristic is visible only through family scores, which are 0 for the Arena, GPT-4-judge, and NBA families and $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts. Under the harness's stated scoring rule (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:details}), a score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller than the influence ordering in that family, and the negative MT-Bench-expert score means it was marginally smaller in at least one case. Because the scoring rule is stated rather than inspectable, and per-case heuristic sizes are not recorded, we read this only as weak evidence that the ordering matches or beats the obvious ``remove the leader's wins'' attack in most cases, but not uniformly.\")\nr(\"Refinements that relinearize the influence after partial removals, or that prune and swap rows within the verified set, were designed but never produced scored evaluations. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:negative}).\",\n  \"Three refined search configurations were designed but none was scored; the cause is not recorded in the evaluation evidence. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:negative}).\")\nr(\"vs.\\\\ direct heuristic (family scores) & Arena $0$, MTB-GPT-4 $0$, NBA $0$ & never worse \\\\\\\\\\n & MTB-expert $-0.0053$ & heuristic smaller in $\\\\ge$1 case \\\\\\\\\",\n  \"vs.\\\\ direct heuristic (family scores) & Arena $0$, MTB-GPT-4 $0$, NBA $0$ & never worse$^\\\\dagger$ \\\\\\\\\\n & MTB-expert $-0.0053$ & heuristic smaller in $\\\\ge$1 case$^\\\\dagger$ \\\\\\\\\")\nr(\"Positive values mean the comparator needed more rows.}\",\n  \"Positive values mean the comparator needed more rows. $^\\\\dagger$Interpretation relies on the harness's stated scoring rule.}\")\n\n# main results readings\nr(\"For LLM evaluation, these counts are within reach of a single motivated actor casting votes or of a modest annotation error.\",\n  \"These are upper bounds: sets of this size provably exist, and smaller ones may.\")\nr(\"Its 62-vote certificate is a smaller share of the data than any other leader's by more than an order of magnitude. Scale buys precision for the average contrast. It does not protect the specific contrast at the top, where only a small share of rows carries information about the leader and runner-up (Eq.~\\\\ref{eq:influence}).\",\n  \"Its 62-vote certificate is a smaller share of the data than any other leader's by more than an order of magnitude. One explanation consistent with Eq.~\\\\eqref{eq:influence} is that a large data set gives precision for the average contrast but not for the specific contrast at the top, if only a small share of rows involves the leader and runner-up. We did not measure that share or the decisive margin, so this explanation is a hypothesis, not a finding.\")\n\n# top-k paragraph\nr(\"The cost is governed by the gap at the boundary being tested, not by the leader's lead. In the NBA seasons, the 5th and 6th teams are close enough that one game reorders them. The evaluator's degenerate-margin check rules out exact ties ($m\\\\le 10\\\\tau$). So ``is the top-$k$ stable?''\",\n  \"A natural hypothesis, again from Eq.~\\\\eqref{eq:influence}, is that the cost depends on the score gap and information at the boundary being tested rather than on the leader's lead; for example, the NBA 5th and 6th teams may be nearly tied. We did not record boundary gaps, so we cannot confirm this. The harness's stated degenerate-margin check excludes only near-exact ties ($m\\\\le 10\\\\tau$). Either way, ``is the top-$k$ stable?''\")\n\n# Discussion: one estimator\nr(\"All results use ridge BT with $\\\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player.\",\n  \"All results use ridge BT with ties counted as half wins and, per the harness specification, $\\\\lambda=0.1$ and a 50-comparison minimum per player.\")\nr(\"The direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. We evaluated one search configuration,\",\n  \"The direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. More generally, comparator, control, and scoring details come from the harness's stated protocol, whose source is not among our artifacts. We evaluated one search configuration,\")\nr(\"Iterative relinearization and set-refinement variants were not evaluated, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open.\",\n  \"Three refined variants were designed but not scored, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open.\")\n\n# SI: derivation\nr(\"The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script\",\n  \"The two seasons sum to 2,630 games; we could not check this against the harness's season file, which is not among our artifacts. The script\")\n\n# SI: details\nr(\"\\\\label{app:details}\\n\",\n  \"\\\\label{app:details}\\n\\nThis subsection records the evaluation harness's stated protocol. The harness source is not among the artifacts accompanying this paper, so these details are reported as specified and could not be inspected. Only the statements about what the finder receives are confirmed by the finder's own interface documentation (\\\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 1--46).\\n\")\nr(\"For each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\",\n  \"For each real source, the harness is stated to load the pinned resource, drop rows shared with the development split, shuffle the rows with a fixed private seed, and iteratively remove players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Players are relabelled per case, so the finder receives only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, labels, or dates (confirmed by the interface documentation). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the harness's own fit.\")\nr(\"Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream (lines 611--626).\",\n  \"Each case and size uses its own fixed random stream.\")\nr(\"The same verified prefix search is applied (lines 595--608).\",\n  \"The same verified prefix search is applied.\")\nr(\"Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected (lines 658--721). The finder returned a size-8 set, equal to the enumerated minimum.\",\n  \"Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected. The finder returned a size-8 set, and the control score of 0 indicates equality with the enumerated minimum under the stated score definition.\")\nr(\"Because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\\\text{infl}}$, a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller. The only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only evidence that the heuristic was smaller anywhere.\",\n  \"If the scoring rule is as stated, then, because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\\\text{infl}}$ (log-ratio 0.0 in every case), a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller, and the only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only indication that it was smaller anywhere. This inference depends on the stated scoring formula, which we could not inspect.\")\n\n# SI negative\nr(\"Three refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. All three relinearize the influence after partial removals and exploit the fact that \\\\eqref{eq:bt} depends only on per-pair counts. The first adds multi-pair targeting and exact pruning. The second adds deterministic refit budgets and a two-for-one swap refinement. The third adds exact pruning seeded from the influence set. None produced a scored evaluation: all three of three were unscored because execution failed before evaluation. Their effect on $\\\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss.\",\n  \"Three refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. As recorded, they are relinearized category-knapsack variants: one with multi-pair targeting and pruning, one with swap refinement, and one with pruning seeded from the baseline influence set. No source for these configurations is among our artifacts, so we give no further implementation detail. All three were unscored (0 of 3 scored), and the evaluation evidence does not record why. Their effect on $\\\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss.\")\n\n# SI repro data\nr(\"All data are public. Arena:\",\n  \"All data are public. The snapshots below are those listed in the task's data specification; that the harness loaded exactly these files, and the split it applied, could not be verified from our artifacts. Arena:\")\nr(\" Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\\\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\",\n  \"\")\nr(\"Python with NumPy 2.2.6 and SciPy 1.15.3 (\\\\nolinkurl{task/requirements.txt}), CPU only.\",\n  \"Python, CPU only; the finder depends only on NumPy and the standard library.\")\nr(\" Evaluator: \\\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}.\", \" The evaluation harness is not included (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:setup}).\")\np.write_text(t)\nprint('ok')\nEOF\npython3 /tmp/edit2.py && grep -n \"evaluate_final\\|split_manifest\\|requirements.txt\\|lines [0-9]\\|held-out\\|execution failed\\|motivated actor\\|evaluator\" paper.tex","description":"Apply results, discussion, and SI edits"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2923,"cache_read_input_tokens":98703,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2923},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35e6a956-3122-4827-a187-45bf94db5b6c","uuid":"1e390292-533a-4bba-84d6-7ccd469ffc32","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:35:28.439Z","request_id":"req_011CgVbCRQAQ2PKVkJ8Kxj7n"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01664qEdvj7Jd7FmdMEkvmKi","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n135:\\caption{Evaluation designs and cases. $n$ is the number of comparison rows after filtering, recovered exactly from the evaluator's reported fraction $\\kver/n$ and the verified size $\\kver$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}). Every source contributes drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$ cases.}\n174:The configuration evaluated here \\emph{is} the influence ordering plus verified prefix search. Its sizes coincide with those of the evaluator's own one-step-influence comparator in every case: the mean $\\log_2$ ratio is 0.0, with CI [0.0, 0.0]. The top-1 drop fractions are equal to the reported precision. The pre-declared test of a strict improvement over this ordering (CI lower bound $>0$) is therefore \\emph{not supported}. We make no claim of algorithmic superiority. Three refined search configurations were designed but none was scored; the cause is not recorded in the evaluation evidence. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\ref{app:negative}).\n253:For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n290:Table~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\n336:This subsection records the evaluation harness's stated protocol. The harness source is not among the artifacts accompanying this paper, so these details are reported as specified and could not be inspected. Only the statements about what the finder receives are confirmed by the finder's own interface documentation (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 1--46).\n351:For real cases, each seed's score is $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(\\min(k_{\\text{infl}},k_{\\text{heur}})/\\kver),-4,4)$. Here $k_{\\text{infl}}$ is the evaluator's one-step-influence comparator, and an unverified proposal scores $-4$. Family scores are weighted means over the family's cases. If the scoring rule is as stated, then, because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\text{infl}}$ (log-ratio 0.0 in every case), a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller, and the only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only indication that it was smaller anywhere. This inference depends on the stated scoring formula, which we could not inspect.\n371:Python, CPU only; the finder depends only on NumPy and the standard library. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n374:Perturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree); BT refit at lines 72--117, influence ordering and verified prefix search at lines 151--217. The evaluation harness is not included (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}). Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35e6a956-3122-4827-a187-45bf94db5b6c","uuid":"f38d884f-0674-49e7-ad0f-b2834b45026c","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:35:28.522Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ok\n135:\\caption{Evaluation designs and cases. $n$ is the number of comparison rows after filtering, recovered exactly from the evaluator's reported fraction $\\kver/n$ and the verified size $\\kver$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}). Every source contributes drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$ cases.}\n174:The configuration evaluated here \\emph{is} the influence ordering plus verified prefix search. Its sizes coincide with those of the evaluator's own one-step-influence comparator in every case: the mean $\\log_2$ ratio is 0.0, with CI [0.0, 0.0]. The top-1 drop fractions are equal to the reported precision. The pre-declared test of a strict improvement over this ordering (CI lower bound $>0$) is therefore \\emph{not supported}. We make no claim of algorithmic superiority. Three refined search configurations were designed but none was scored; the cause is not recorded in the evaluation evidence. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\ref{app:negative}).\n253:For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n290:Table~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\n336:This subsection records the evaluation harness's stated protocol. The harness source is not among the artifacts accompanying this paper, so these details are reported as specified and could not be inspected. Only the statements about what the finder receives are confirmed by the finder's own interface documentation (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 1--46).\n351:For real cases, each seed's score is $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(\\min(k_{\\text{infl}},k_{\\text{heur}})/\\kver),-4,4)$. Here $k_{\\text{infl}}$ is the evaluator's one-step-influence comparator, and an unverified proposal scores $-4$. Family scores are weighted means over the family's cases. If the scoring rule is as stated, then, because the evaluated finder reproduces $k_{\\text{infl}}$ (log-ratio 0.0 in every case), a family score of 0 means the heuristic was never smaller, and the only nonzero family score, $-0.0053$ for MT-Bench experts, is the only indication that it was smaller anywhere. This inference depends on the stated scoring formula, which we could not inspect.\n371:Python, CPU only; the finder depends only on NumPy and the standard library. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n374:Perturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree); BT refit at lines 72--117, influence ordering and verified prefix search at lines 151--217. The evaluation harness is not included (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}). Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":true}}
